The Book of Love
Finding your power through love
By Catalina Butnaru
Introduction
The Power of Love is a reflective memoir on love as a life force. It’s the book that answers the one question that has been one everyone’s mind: “what is love?”; but it does so without reducing love to an emotion. 


Inquiring into the nature of love isn’t easy. It’s a big and beautiful question that remains unanswered until now. 


The British Library holds over two hundred million titles on any topic under the sun, but love. I say this because I found countless volumes on intelligence, consciousness, and the origins of life, but almost nothing about love; at least not on the shelves of serious research and inquiry.


The Book of Love changes everything. It looks at the nature of love from a love to be a fundamental life force, not merely an emotion. The power of love is that it dissolves the boundaries of separateness between us and allows our spirit to expand through connection with another. Love pushes us to grow and this growth is its very nature: it’s a force of expansion.


I argue that love expands what theoretical biologist Stuart Kauffman calls “the adjacent possible” – the new version of us that emerges when we step beyond familiar contexts. By drawing us into affinity with another person, purpose or domain, love asks us to explore new ways of being and enlarges the scope of our existence.
To inquire into love as a life force, I look beyond the emotions it evokes and explore it through a multi-disciplinary perspective, bringing evidence from neuroscience, depth psychology, mysticism, and philosophy, to show that love is not secondary to consciousness, but one with it.
Consider the musician who pours herself into her craft: her thoughts, habits, and sensitivities are reorganized by the love for music, and in turn, the musical domain itself evolves through the musician’s mastery. This is how, I believe, love drives evolution, not through natural selection, but through meaning-making.
After examining love’s nature and purpose, I turn to a more personal question: if love is fundamental to life, why does it sometimes empower us and at other times devastate us?
I suggest this is a false paradox. Love itself never wounds; what hurts are distortions: power struggles, manipulative dynamics, and cultural scripts that confuse control with connection.
In the chapters that follow, I explore how love and power intertwine across five dimensions: the love of self, the love of another, the love of humanity, the love of life, and the love of God.
But before that, I share a personal revelation on love that changed everything I knew about love. After years of trauma, heartbreak, and despair caused by overtly abusive relationships or by cumulated micro-traumas in relating to others, I nearly lost my life force. Nothing seemed to help me recover from the sea of despondency where I had capsized. Then, when I nearly gave up trying altogether, something outstanding happened. 
In a moment that can only be called mystical, I felt my entire being flooded with love: peaceful, warm, all-encompassing. This is how, I believe, love saved me. That experience saved me. It revealed a truth that became the seed of this book: love itself is never the problem; it is the misuse of power that causes pain.
From there, my mission became clear, to help others disentangle love from its distortions and substitutes so that we can experience its full, life-giving power. 
Love’s substitutes are everywhere: self-gratification disguised as self-care, fantasy mistaken for intimacy, and self-improvement sold as self-love. I know these patterns well. For years, I tried to perfect myself into worthiness — analyzing, labeling, optimizing — until I realized that self-love isn’t about becoming someone better, but about finally loving who you are.
From the love of self I briefly move to the love of life, where I challenge the myth that life’s worth can be maximized through a set of curated or upgraded lifestyles. I reflect both on my own attempts to love life in a generation that treats the “good life” like a product: something to be planned, hacked, aesthetically produced, bought, or performed.
And lastly, I explore the love of God, because it’s how I came to experience unconditional love. The love of God taught me peace and connection with humanity. As I describe my experience of love through understanding divinity (the numinous), I also offer some practical steps you can take to rest in love without seeking to understand whether it comes from God or from a regulated nervous system, as a result of prayer and meditation.
This book does not debate religion or spirituality. Instead, it illuminates how any contemplative practice rooted in love, like Sufism, Buddhism, or humanism, can elevate our aliveness and connect us with our own Higher Power.
And so, in exploring the five expressions of love – its essence and its distortions – I hope to reveal what empowers and what does not. By the end of this book, my wish is that readers come to see love as I have: not as a fragile feeling, but as a vital force: an unstoppable current that fulfills life, strengthens the soul, and helps us grow.


Chapter outlines
Chapter 1: The nature of love
This book begins with the story of how I came to write it. I have had many encounters with love, but only one irrepeatable revelation — one that compelled me to write. The origin story of my book matters because it shows it wasn’t driven by ambition, but of a pursuit of truth. It’s the story of how I became convinced that love is a fundamental force in life, not just an emotion.
My first encounter with love came at the age of four. I can only describe it as a peak experience — a moment of powerful self-awareness, where warmth and kindness filled my entire being. It felt as though I stood before God: fragile and separate, yet completely held. Love, then, began as bliss, but it turned sour quickly. 
From then until my forties it felt like love encounters became more and more painful and bewildering. Years of grief brought on by unkind and unloving relationships, severed my connection to my life force and I nearly gave up. And then, when nothing seemed to work, I surrendered once more, just like when I was a child. I opened my heart, not to anyone or anything, but simply to love itself, and that’s what saved me from crippling depression.
This second mystical experience, in adulthood, became my revelation: love is never the problem. It is our perception of love that distorts it. 
I also discuss how love is not separate from consciousness, nor secondary to it. Love is consciousness. Love is not reducible to attachment or instinct; it is the very root of our life force, always energising, never draining. 
Chapter 2: The purpose of love
If love is like a life force, what is its purpose? In this chapter I look at love’s purpose more deeply: to help us become more expansive, wiser, and more fulfilled versions of ourselves. This may sound poetic, but it’s verifiable in experience: we grow through love. Whenever we love deeply – a person, a craft, a flower, an animal or a little corner of the world – we expand.


If I had to bring sturdier evidence, I’d turn to information science, to a concept called the ‘Markov blanket’, a term coined by Judea Pearl to describe the boundary through which the outer turns into an inner model of reality. This boundary filters light, sound, touch, and pressure into meaningful signals that help us survive.\


In humans, these blankets are not only sensory, they’re also psychological. They include our beliefs and assumptions, and the stories we tell ourselves about how the world works. These stories keep us comfortable, wrapped in a blanket of potentially useful, but also limiting interpretations of our life and others’.


Love covers our hearts with a different kind of blanket: one that keeps us fiery and courageous in the face of adversity, one that makes the ‘outer world’ feel much more exciting to explore and our outdated beliefs easy to get rid of for a loved one.  


When we look at the purpose of love through the lens of information theory, it’s as if love compels us to soak up the world and expand what we’re capable of processing, so to say. We want to understand the world of another and pursue that which seems unreachable. Love awakens an insatiable curiosity about people and domains, bridges differences, forges unlikely alliances, and makes us capable of knowing more than we ever thought we’d know. It is with love, that consciousness evolves, it’s through connection, rather than random selection.


Chapter 3: The love of another – What happens when you have the power to love
This chapter looks at the power that romantic love can have over ourselves and loved ones, making it the perfect battleground for power dynamics – some wounding, some healthily challenging. 
To understand the life force behind romantic love, and ultimately its power, we need to go beyond defining romantic love through the lens of attachment theories and marital relations. What’s needed is a short introduction to the archetype of love which defines love as a force that surges from the deepest and most energizing part of our psyche – the libido, the eros. When romantic love activates from the depth of our soul, very few things can stop us from achieving out desires. That’s why pure love can look like fierce protection and devotion or obsession. Both are fueled by the libido, the life force behind romantic love, which is often misused or mis-channeled once the relationship starts to blossom. 
Here I’ll list 
A brief look at history helps set the stage. [a]Our great-grandparents often saw the misuse of power in unequal, arranged marriages, while today’s couples wrestle with disempowering romance in the form of chaotic situationships. Across generations, however, the same problem persists: the distribution of power in relationships is often unequal which leads to suffering.
To help readers understand why romantic love in particular can become a playground for unequal displays of power, [b][c][d]I draw on depth psychology, where romantic love is defined as a universal archetype. When activated, [e]this archetype surges from the unconscious with immense energy, needing to assert itself. [f]In this process of assertion, the psychic energy stirs up unaddressed or unprocessed content from the unconscious, which most often gets expelled (projected) onto a romantic partner, who all to often becomes either a target of unaddressed rage or a source of destabilizing triggers. If unaddressed, the projections exchange that plays out in romantic love gets amplified in power plays, where one partner dominates while the other diminishes. 
Millennials and Gen Z are probably [g]the first generations to arduously study the toxic power dynamic that plays out in romantic relationships. The rise of terms like ghosting, breadcrumbing, and relationship abuse since 2020 reflect an increased awareness and scrutiny. [h][i][j]Love is no longer seen only as safe and nurturing; it is increasingly perceived as a threat to one’s mental wellbeing and sense of self. 
In this chapter, I argue that the solution [k]is not to withdraw from love, nor to retaliate with reverse power tactics. The answer [l]is to restore love to its rightful role: to bring power back into relationships not as domination, but as mutual empowerment. I call this relational sovereignty: the practice of sharing power within love, and it is here that I will include a framework for cultivating it[m], grounded in modern practices that have helped me heal and build health in my own relationship.
Chapter 4: The love of self – Master your power through self-love        
From The love of another I turn to The love of self, a concept I intend to challenge and revise in this chapter. I begin by dissecting the cultural trope of self-love, often wrapped in well-meaning but misleading advice like, “To love another, you must first love yourself.” At first glance, it [n][o][p]sounds empowering, but it carries a false causality: that loving yourself more will automatically make you better at loving others. Reality is far more complex, and given how often we conflate self-love with self-indulgence, “loving ourselves harder” can just as easily turn us into insufferable narcissists.
The recent surge of self-love practices has little to do with truly learning how to love oneself and everything to do with our collective disappointment in romantic love as situationships [q]collapse left and right. In this chapter, I argue that the obsession with self-love is more disempowering than the absence of self-love. To support this claim, I draw on my analysis of hundreds of reviews from therapy and self-help platforms [r]including X, Y and Z. The results were unmistakable: after exhausting every tool, app, and practice imaginable, people come to the same realization—self-love is never about the method, the program, or the content. What makes the difference is honest inner work. In this chapter, I will unpack what that means and show how to distinguish genuine love of self from the kinds of practices that quietly drain your power rather than amplify it.
Chapter 5: The love of life – The source of power in one’s own life
This chapter continues the exploration of love and power by turning to one of the most foundational forms of love: the love of life itself. When life is good, loving it feels effortless and we easily equate the love of life with pleasure, joy, or comfort. But when sorrow arrives, we revise the definition[s]. Suddenly, the love of life becomes about finding purpose, or enduring with stoicism. Both approaches, however, are conditional; they tie our love of life to something beyond life itself – whether pleasure or resilience in the face of fate.
In this chapter, I argue that the love of life cannot be conditional, because life itself is far too unpredictable for any condition to reliably hold. As the saying goes, “the only constant in life is change.” Life delivers joy and suffering, purpose and emptiness, curveballs and dry spells, often in no predictable order.
I’ll share some of my own reflections on navigating change, including when I turned away from atheism to embrace deep spiritual practices, which resulted in me learning that fiercely loving and living your own life does not shield you from being profoundly humbled. When I grew weary of hyper-independence, I realized that the love of life means little if it is not meaningfully shared. And when I shifted from trying to fix the world to allowing it to unfold as it was, I discovered that happiness cannot be forced by the push of one’s will[t]. These radical shifts in my own existential odyssey taught me that the love of life cannot rest on pleasure, purpose, or control – it can only rest on my capacity to change.[u]
The love of life is the ability to meet change with a sense of victory and expansion. Each time you learn a new skill, shift a perspective, feel emotions deeply, or master them with maturity, each time you step into new roles as a parent, partner, or friend – you live through a small victory in the midst of constant change. And every such victory expands and enriches our consciousness. To love these victories over change is to love life itself. 


Chapter 6: The love of humanity – How to lift people to power


In this chapter, I position the love of humanity not as a virtuous and moral “force of good”, but as a profound source of individual and collective power. Being a “good person” is not the proof that the love of humanity requires, what the love of humanity requires is for you to be oneself fully and powerfully, yet with the awareness that authentic selfhood must add to and expand the ways in which others live when you’re around. To love humanity is not to perform goodness, but to enrich what it means to be human, both for oneself and for others.


The love of humanity has two requirements. [v]The first is to love all ways of being human: all cultures, lifestyles, beliefs, abilities, and atypical ways of processing experience or expressing one’s inner life into the world. The second requirement is to release our obsession with the laws of power: how to influence, how to dominate, how to win. Both obsessions – whether with collective identity and labels or with social power – drain our energy. They diminish our personal life force either by trapping us in zero-sum games with one another or by dissolving our life force into a collectively accepted identity.


As an expat, I’ve learned to love the myriad ways of being human through traveling to more than forty-five countries, living in multicultural communities, and practicing small but powerful acts of unassuming kindness, curiosity, and respect. Traveling confronted me with my own biases, while living among people of many backgrounds reflected how I resisted being reduced to a label. Yet that resistance didn’t come from pride or judgement, but from a desire to belong. That’s because I never quite felt like I belonged anywhere. Up until my mid-thirties, I believed I had Gypsy roots, so most of the time I would unconsciously cast myself as an outcast, a wanderer, someone forever on the margins of an established and accepted community. When I later discovered that my ancestry traced back not to the Romani, but to the ancient Indo-European Dacians, I realized how much energy I had spent resisting or embracing identities that the world didn’t approve of. 


In hindsight, being a self-cast outcast shaped my choices in unexpected ways. I deliberately sought friendships and love across cultures and ethnicities, challenging myself to dissolve resistance and bias to inter-personal differences. Over time, I came to love and cherish differences in others, while standing up firmly for my individuality.
Through personal storytelling and reflection on how my generation and I have struggled with authenticity, the message of this chapter is that the love of humanity cannot be practiced as a moral duty to “always do the right thing.” Our loyalties – to self, family, tribe, or nation – often override fairness and morality. Instead, the love of humanity must be practiced as the willingness to allow, encourage, and even celebrate the full freedom of being human.
Chapter 7: The love of God – Know your higher power
This final chapter turns to love from a spiritual perspective. As a former atheist turned agnostic, and later a believer, I have spent years wrestling with the idea of God. I questioned faith, sought proof, and weighed every revelation against both reason and heart. What began as a quest for evidence that God exists, slowly transformed into a quiet knowing: not through scientific inquiry, but through acts of love toward myself, humanity, and life itself.
Looking back on my life, I recognize that my understanding of God has been shaped both by the people I encountered in ordinary life and by the mystics and scholars who illuminated my path, including: Georgij Gurdjieff, Manly P. Hall, Ervin László, Seyyed Hossein Nasr, and Don Miguel Ruiz. Everyday encounters taught me to practice love in the mundane, while sacred teachings awakened me to the mystery that underlies all existence. Yet the deepest revelations about love and God have come not from intellect, but from the heart; and this chapter is where I share these revelations.[w][x][y]
In this final chapter, I also describe the love of God as the highest expression of our capacity to experience the divine. Some souls know God without either indoctrination or practice; others feel distant from the possibility that God exists altogether. Despite these differences, no one is more correct than another. However, it’s when we take these differences too seriously that we make our inquiry into divinity disempowering. I also reflect on how easy it is to lose oneself in spiritual practices that promise transcendence, yet distance us from the divine. These are practices designed by people for people, rather than practices that spring from authentic devotion, love, and revelation. True devotion, I’ve come to see, is not submission to hollow practices, but a genuine appreciation of the God within all things.
Because this book is about love as a source of power, I dwell less on defining God’s nature and more on how I learned to find strength in my love of God: first as someone who believed she had to earn worthiness through purification and discipline, and finally as someone who discovered that she was — and always had been — fully worthy of divine love.
My hope is that this final chapter helps readers see that the love of God is not empowering because it promises reward for goodness, but because it gathers all other forms of love (the love of self, the love of another, the love of humanity, and the love of life) into one unifying current. In that realization, we come to understand what mystics have revealed for millennia: that through love, we awaken to our oneness with God. Love is the unifying field through which all life moves, and the expansive force that moves through all living things. And so my argument comes full circle: love is the ultimate power — not metaphorically, not sentimentally, but fundamentally. It is the force that binds, expands, and evolves all of life. 
